("Tickets, hooks and streams", "Per-device ownership and normal reset are sound under the single legacy-default-stream contract: CheckedDeviceGuard selects q's device, the recursive mutex covers growth/enqueue, new slots are zeroed, and the last atomicInc wraps at NC-1. A rejected launch that starts no CTA takes no ticket; a sticky execution fault does not promise context recovery. The hook timeout synchronizes then resets merge slots before throwing; both EB3/EB4 pass that pointer/count. The choose kernel has completed beforehand, or choose tickets are absent in default CHOOSE_SITE=1. Retained FH LF5 has 11 successful checks; its same-process next-output bytes are not retained, so that particular fresh-process equality is receipt-only. Build F has 99 dynamic exports, zero hook-symbol matches, no device EarlyCounterHook signatures, 13 real spo2 entries and no hook/timeout strings. FH has 34 device hook signatures and two hook methods among 15 entries. Ordinary nm reports stripped host symbols, so it cannot establish hidden bridge symbols. All launches use stream 0; Python exposes no alternate stream. Unsupported concurrent external/non-default streams may race; they are out of contract. Actual nvcc -E --default-stream per-thread fails at the new #error; legacy preprocessing succeeds. Read eb3.cuh:91-164,568-607,811-869,944-947,1102 and eb4.cuh:207-218,719-720,824; ran 024,027,028,033,035."),
